.idea/
node_modules/
.env

# The two manual runbooks under setup/. They are working notes rather than project files: they carry
# real database users, their passwords and the internal hostnames those live on, written out ready to
# paste into a shell. Nothing in the repo reads them, so untracking costs nothing, and the remote is
# public — a tracked copy is one push away from publishing every credential in it. Keep them on disk,
# keep them out of git.
setup/mongodb.js
setup/redis.txt

# Visual studio
.vscode
.vs

# Semgrep's own output (see semgrep/README.md)
semgrep.sarif

# `yarn test:cov` output, gated at 100% on every metric in vitest.config.mjs and read again by
# qodana.sh under SKIP_TESTS=1. This comment used to say the number was "inspected, not gated";
# the two unit suites closed the branches the replay could not reach and the threshold went in.
coverage/

# Stryker (yarn test:mutation)
reports/
.stryker-tmp/

# Qodana — scan results (SARIF + report), written by .githooks/pre-commit and pre-push.
# They pass --results-dir .qodana/results so the SARIF is readable from the repo root
# after a failed gate, rather than under ~/.cache/JetBrains/Qodana/<project-hash>/.
.qodana/
# GitNexus index storage. The committed part is .gitnexusrc at the repo root.
.gitnexus/

# Core dumps. Qodana's JS language service aborts on its 1 GB heap cap inside the scan container,
# and while the host's core_pattern is relative the kernel drops a ~1.3 GB `core.<pid>` here in the
# repo root — untracked, unignored, and large enough that `prettier --check .` fails on it with
# "Invalid string length" and takes the whole commit gate down. qodana.sh sweeps them after every
# scan; this is the second lock, so a crash costs a diagnosis rather than a red gate.
core
core.*
